{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_1","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Tokyo Skytree height\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Tokyo Skytree\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tokyo_Skytree\"},{\"title\":\"Tokyo Skytree\",\"url\":\"https://wikipedia.com/wiki/Sky_Tree\"},{\"title\":\"Tokyo Sky Tree\",\"url"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"459e5cda-874b-444f-92f3-0b54d1c92b94","uuid":"f4773e7c-2031-4f74-aa45-10976fa31426","timestamp":"2026-10-31T20:10:33.081Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Tokyo Skytree height","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_1","content":[{"title":"Tokyo Skytree","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tokyo_Skytree"},{"title":"Tokyo Skytree","url":"https://wikipedia.com/wiki/Sky_Tree"},{"title":"Tokyo Sky Tree","url":"https://www.skyscrapercenter.com/building/wd/9107"}]},"The Tokyo Skytree is 634 metres (2,080 feet) tall. Its full height was reached in early 2011, and it has been the tallest tower in Japan since opening in 2012. Guinness has certified it as the world's tallest tower, and Britannica describes it as the world's tallest freestanding tower.\n\nThe number was partly a deliberate choice. Sources say it can be read in Japanese as \"Musashi,\" the name of an ancient region that included what is now Tokyo.\n\nOne source claims the Skytree is the second-tallest structure in the world, behind the Burj Khalifa. I'd treat that claim with caution, since it appears in a low-quality source and I didn't verify it."],"durationSeconds":5.607212542,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
